<h2>Cast-in-place concrete pipe</h2>
<p>Cast-in-place concrete pipe, abbreviated CIPCP, is a way of building large pipe without hauling precast sections to the site. A trench is cut to a rounded profile, so that the earth itself becomes the outer form for the bottom of the pipe. A machine then travels along the trench placing concrete against the soil and shaping the inside with a form, leaving a continuous, jointless concrete conduit. The American Concrete Institute publishes the governing specification, ACI 346, for earth-supported cast-in-place concrete pipe up to 120 inches in diameter.</p>
<p>The method is used almost entirely for conveying water: irrigation canals and laterals, agricultural drainage and storm drainage, especially in the western states where long runs of large pipe through stable soil are common. It is new construction, done in an open trench by heavy civil or irrigation contractors. It has nothing to do with repairing the sewer under your house.</p>

<h2>Cured in place pipe</h2>
<p><a href="/cured-in-place-pipe">Cured in place pipe</a> is a rehabilitation method. The pipe already exists and has failed: cracked clay, corroded cast iron, root-invaded joints. A flexible tube soaked in resin is inserted through an existing opening, pressed against the inside of the old pipe and hardened in place with hot water, steam or ultraviolet light. When it cures, there is a new structural pipe inside the old one, with no joints and no trench. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/installation">cured in place pipe installation</a> page walks through the day; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/cost">cost guide</a> shows what it has saved homeowners against digging.</p>
<p>CIPP is governed by ASTM standards, chiefly F1216 for inversion, F1743 for pulled-in-place liners, F2019 for UV-cured glass liners and D5813 for the material, and it is performed by specialist lining contractors with their own equipment and manufacturer training.</p>

<h2>Where the confusion comes from</h2>
<p>"Cast" and "cured" are both things you do to a material that starts soft and ends hard, and "in place" appears in both names. Add the fact that most people hear these terms from a contractor rather than read them, and the swap is easy to make. Search engines, which rank on what people type, then return both. The result is that a homeowner with a broken sewer searches "cast in place pipe installers" and lands among irrigation contractors, while an irrigation district engineer occasionally finds a sewer lining company.</p>

<h2>Which one do you need?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A pipe under your property is cracked, leaking, root-invaded or backing up</strong>, and you want it fixed without a trench: you need <strong>cured in place pipe</strong>. Start with <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/near-me">cured in place pipe near me</a> to find a verified installer, or <a href="/all-contractors">browse contractors by state</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>You are building a new large-diameter irrigation or drainage line</strong> through open ground: you need <strong>cast-in-place concrete pipe</strong> or a precast alternative, and a heavy civil contractor. This site does not list them.</li>
  <li><strong>You have an existing concrete storm or sewer line that is failing</strong>, cast-in-place or precast: cured in place lining may well be an option, depending on diameter and condition. A lining contractor can tell you after a camera inspection; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/repair">cured in place pipe repair</a> page explains the criteria.</li>
</ul>

